+### Append storage
+
+Each append remains its own version entry and keeps the complete original
+operation: the fragment, version metadata, extras, envelope facts, the
+`message_serial`, `version_serial`, `history_serial` and `delivery_serial`
+identities, and any `op_id` receipt. Only the accumulated string is stored
+once. Consecutive appends of one message form an *append run*; the run has a
+single snapshot holding the accumulated string at its newest version, and each
+entry records the byte length of its own accumulated data. Because an append
+only concatenates, that data is exactly the snapshot prefix of that length. An
+update, a delete, an import, or an append the store cannot place in the current
+run is stored self-contained; the next append starts a new run.
+
+Every read reconstructs the same full-state record as before: `get_latest`,
+version-history pages in both directions, replay, cold recovery, rewind,
+history substitution, and idempotent `op_id` replays. Wire payloads, serials,
+cursors and ordering are unchanged. Reconstruction fails closed: a missing
+snapshot, a length outside it, a split inside a UTF-8 character, or a snapshot
+that does not end the prefix with the entry's own fragment is an error, never a
+record with partial data.
+
+For `n` appends of `f` bytes, retained data grows as `n·f` plus one snapshot
+per run instead of one complete accumulated string per version (`f·n²/2`,
+stored twice with the envelope copy). Each append still writes the current
+aggregate once: backends with a latest-state record keep it, and the run
+snapshot is extended in the same atomic commit. PostgreSQL, MySQL and SurrealDB
+concatenate the fragment server-side; DynamoDB and ScyllaDB cannot append to a
+stored binary value and rewrite the snapshot.
+
+| Backend | Run snapshot | Removed when |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Memory | Per-message string next to the version chain | Its last retained entry is purged |
+| PostgreSQL, MySQL | `<version_entries_table>_runs` row; entries carry its id in `append_run` so one statement returns a page and its snapshots | Purge finds no retained entry of the message within the run |
+| DynamoDB | `__append_run__ <message> <run>` item in the entries table, without index keys | TTL, refreshed on every append past all of its entries; never, once an `op_id` receipt refers to it (receipts do not expire) |
+| ScyllaDB | `r:<message> <run>` row in the commit partition; `append_run`, `append_len` and `append_head` columns on the latest row point at it | Not expired, like the other commit rows |
+| SurrealDB | `<prefix>_version_append_runs` record, stored as a string | Purge finds no retained entry within the run; runs referenced by receipts are kept with the receipts |
+
+Upgrading needs no backfill. The first startup creates the run table and adds
+the nullable `append_run` entry column (PostgreSQL, MySQL; both are
+metadata-only changes), creates the SurrealDB run table, or adds three nullable
+ScyllaDB commit columns, so the database role needs DDL permission once.
+DynamoDB needs no schema change. Existing entries stay as written and remain
+readable; the first append to an existing message starts a run. Stores that
+keep a complete latest-state record (DynamoDB, ScyllaDB, SurrealDB) store the
+run pointer next to it together with the version it describes, and use it only
+while that version is still the latest. Include run tables and items
+in consistent backups, and do not edit or delete run snapshots independently of
+their entries: entries whose snapshot is missing cannot be read.
+
+Older releases reject compact entries with a decode error rather than returning
+a record without data. During a rolling upgrade, finish upgrading every node
+before older nodes serve reads or appends of streams that newer nodes have
+extended. On PostgreSQL and MySQL an older node also cannot mutate a message
+whose latest version is compact; DynamoDB, ScyllaDB and SurrealDB older nodes
+still read the complete latest-state record but fail on history pages and
+replays containing compact entries. To roll back, stop writers and run
+`sockudo --config <file> --materialize-append-storage`, which rewrites every
+compact entry and receipt of the configured version store as a self-contained
+record (restoring the per-version storage cost) before the older release
+starts. Compact entries also disappear on their own once retention removes them.
+
 `op_id` is scoped by app, channel, message, and action. Replaying the same ID
 with the same canonical operation returns the original acknowledgement without
 fanout. Reusing it with a different operation is an idempotency conflict.
